== Thermodynamic configuration ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Parameter !! Value
|-
| Working fluid || Calorically perfect ideal air
|-
| Gas constants || R = 287.0 J/(kg K), Cp = 1004.5 J/(kg K), Cv = 717.5 J/(kg K)
|-
| Cold reservoir || 268.15 K (-5 °C)
|-
| Hot reservoir || 298.15 K (25 °C)
|-
| Initial filling state || 1.000 bar absolute at 298.15 K
|-
| Total gas inventory || 0.08464 kg
|-
| Rotational speed || 17.90 rpm
|-
| Valve model || Continuous ideal pressure-driven diode, zero threshold
|}

The simulated state is
<code>X = (m_S,U_S,m_L,U_L,m_C,U_C,m_H,U_H)</code>. Pressure and temperature
are reconstructed from mass and internal energy. Heat transfer obeys
<code>Qdot = UA (T_reservoir - T_HX)</code>; no isothermal transformation is
imposed.

== Geometry and imposed kinematics ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Element !! Minimum volume !! Maximum volume / swept volume
|-
| Small cylinder S || 0.5544 L || 55.9944 L / 55.44 L swept
|-
| Large cylinder L || 0.6600 L || 66.6600 L / 66.00 L swept
|-
| Cold exchanger C || colspan="2" | 0.800 L fixed gas volume
|-
| Hot exchanger H || colspan="2" | 0.800 L fixed gas volume
|}

Both cylinder clearance volumes are 1% of swept volume. The two mechanisms are
geometrically identical mirror assemblies driven by the same physical crank
pin. They have opposite four-bar assembly branches and opposite normal output
coordinates. They are therefore reflected, not two omitted independent
mechanisms. Different cylinder volumes result from different piston bores; both
physical strokes are 0.4380 m.

{| class="wikitable"
! Mechanical dimension !! Small assembly !! Large assembly
|-
| Ground pivot distance || 0.4840 m || 0.4840 m
|-
| Shared crank radius || colspan="2" | 0.1936 m
|-
| Coupler length || 0.4840 m || 0.4840 m
|-
| Rocker length || 0.5663 m || 0.5663 m
|-
| Output coordinates in rocker frame || (0.3167, -0.4695) m || (0.3167, +0.4695) m
|-
| Four-bar assembly branch || +1 || -1
|-
| Piston connecting-rod length || 2.1902 m || 2.1902 m
|-
| Piston stroke || 0.4380 m || 0.4380 m
|-
| Implied piston bore || 0.4014 m || 0.4380 m
|}

The common crank has an angular offset of 304.375° relative to the solver's
thermodynamic angle origin. This is one common angular orientation, not an
independent phase shift between the two mechanisms. Horizontal slider axes are
centered on each output point's transverse excursion by the declared
construction rule. The thermodynamic solver receives only the resulting volume
laws and their derivatives.

'''Optimization scope:''' this work found a strong candidate within the
restricted family of identical reflected mechanisms. It did not prove that
mechanical symmetry is globally optimal. Coupler length, rocker length, fixed
pivot, output point, piston rod and slider layout were not varied independently
for S and L. Both mechanisms may be independently parameterized in a future
search while retaining the single shared crank pin.

== Heat transfer and hydraulic closure ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Parameter !! Value
|-
| Cold UA || 226.0 W/K
|-
| Hot UA || 226.0 W/K
|-
| L–H effective CdA || 1.654 × 10<sup>-3</sup> m²
|-
| S–C effective CdA || 2.954 × 10<sup>-3</sup> m²
|-
| H→S valve effective CdA || 1.654 × 10<sup>-3</sup> m²
|-
| C→L valve effective CdA || 5.907 × 10<sup>-3</sup> m²
|}

The compressible-orifice law selects the real upstream state from the pressure
gradient and includes choked and unchoked flow. The two passive valves prohibit
reverse flow. CdA is an effective coefficient-area product, not yet a complete
passage geometry.

